# Environment setup notes

Steps for this repo (from repo root, all commands cross-platform):

1. Install uv
- Linux: curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
- Windows: powershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"
- verify: uv --version

2. Pin project metadata (creates pyproject.toml + .python-version, no scaffolding files)
uv init --bare --python 3.12

3. Create the environment
uv venv
(reads version from .python-version; or force with uv venv --python 3.12)

4. Sanity check
.venv/bin/python src/kelwyn.py --run        # Windows: .venv\Scripts\python.exe src\kelwyn.py --run

5. Create kelwyn.sh launcher
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"
[ -d .venv ] || { echo "venv missing: run 'uv venv'" >&2; exit 1; }
exec .venv/bin/python src/kelwyn.py "$@"
chmod +x kelwyn.sh
Windows equivalent kelwyn.bat: @echo off / "%~dp0.venv\Scripts\python.exe" "%~dp0src\kelwyn.py" %*

6. Add dependencies whenever needed: uv add <package> (updates pyproject.toml + uv.lock); stdlib-only now, so nothing yet.

7. Commit pyproject.toml, .python-version, uv.lock (after first uv add), and the launcher scripts. Confirm .venv/ is ignored (it's in your .gitignore already).

8. After cloning elsewhere: uv sync recreates .venv exactly.

From inside the project directory, uv targets the project's .venv automatically:
uv add requests          # installs into .venv + records in pyproject.toml (recommended)
uv pip install requests   # installs into .venv without recording (pip-style)

For a different/unnamed environment, point uv at it explicitly:
uv venv --python 3.12 .venv-312
uv pip install --python .venv-312/bin/python requests     # Windows: .venv-312\Scripts\python.exe

Rule of thumb: uv add when inside the project (keeps lockfile in sync), uv pip install --python <path> for any venv outside the default one.

